WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.960
James Bond is one of the all-time great&nbsp;
movie characters: he's suave, he's tough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.960
and he's got a fashion sense to die for.&nbsp;
But what's the real story behind the scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.360
of Britain's most iconic film franchise?&nbsp;
This is the untold truth of James Bond.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.520
Before there were the James Bond movies,&nbsp;
there were the James Bond novels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.520
Written by Ian Fleming, the 14 Bond books&nbsp;
were a kind of fantasy version of Fleming's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.640
real-life experiences as a member of British Naval&nbsp;
Intelligence. The author was a total unknown when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.280
Casino Royale was set to be published, but he was&nbsp;
determined to make his book a hit, and routinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.000
sent out signed copies of his book to as many&nbsp;
newspaper and magazine editors as he could.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.240
Despite his efforts, however, the book still&nbsp;
wasn't selling well — especially in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.040
But everything changed when Fleming came across&nbsp;
the perfect opportunity to get his name out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.920
After the Suez Crisis, the British Prime Minister&nbsp;
Anthony Eden's health had begun to deteriorate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.920
and he soon found himself in need of a place&nbsp;
to relax. Always one to take an opportunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.840
Fleming eagerly offered the politician access&nbsp;
to Goldeneye, his secluded home in Jamaica.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.560
The author was thrilled by the visit, hoping&nbsp;
Eden's stay at Goldeneye would boost the sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.480
of his book. And believe it or not, the&nbsp;
crazy publicity stunt actually worked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.320
thrusting Fleming into the public eye and&nbsp;
making his stories famous around the world.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.680
Growing up in New York, Albert "Cubby" Broccoli&nbsp;
worked on his uncle's farm, which primarily grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.520
the family's signature vegetable, broccoli.&nbsp;
But young Cubby didn't want to spend his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.320
entire life working in the dirt, so he took his&nbsp;
chances and moved to Hollywood in the 1930s.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.720
After slowly working his way up the industry,&nbsp;
Broccoli ended up co-producing the first feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.520
film adaptation of Ian Fleming's James Bond spy&nbsp;
novels. During his search for the titular hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.800
Broccoli heard an actor named Sean Connery&nbsp;
might be a good choice for the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.240
so he went to see the Scotsman in&nbsp;
Darby O'Gill and the Little People.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.360
Broccoli was impressed with Connery's&nbsp;
work on the movie, but wondered if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.600
actor had the raw sex appeal needed for Bond&nbsp;
— so he showed his his wife the film, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.600
She, of course, made it clear that Connery&nbsp;
was more than capable in this department.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.360
Though the movie's studio, United Artists,&nbsp;
threw around a number of names for the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.240
Broccoli fought for Connery and won — and so began&nbsp;
one of the longest running film series in history.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.194
"I admire your luck, Mr…" "Bond. James Bond."

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.320
Though Sean Connery is typically known as the&nbsp;
first proper Bond, two other actors actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.280
played the role before him. In 1954, CBS&nbsp;
paid Fleming $1,000 for the rights to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.240
Casino Royale and turned the novel into a&nbsp;
one-hour TV special for the series Climax!

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.360
For the first glimpse of Bond in&nbsp;
the flesh, they cast Barry Nelson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.160
an actor who has starred in a ton of TV shows in&nbsp;
his time. But you'll probably recognize him best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.200
as Stuart Ullman, the guy who hires Jack Nicholson&nbsp;
to take care of the Overlook Hotel in The Shining.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.680
After the TV version, a South African radio&nbsp;
station produced an adaptation of Moonraker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.640
featuring the vocal talents of Bob Holness. If&nbsp;
you're American, that name might not mean much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.080
but if you're from the UK, you'd know&nbsp;
him as the host of various game shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.240
particularly of the long running, Trivial&nbsp;
Pursuit-like show Blockbusters. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.160
the idea of game show hosts playing James&nbsp;
Bond doesn't seem to have become a tradition.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.120
So, there's been an Irish Bond. There's been a&nbsp;
Scottish Bond. There was even an Australian Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.000
albeit briefly. But to this day, an American&nbsp;
has never donned the tuxedo for a feature film…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
although one came incredibly close.&nbsp;
After filming For Your Eyes Only,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.800
Roger Moore was ready to&nbsp;
turn in his license to kill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.200
and James Brolin was one of the top choices&nbsp;
to replace the British star for Octopussy.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.840
Despite his American accent, Brolin perfected his&nbsp;
British-isms so well that he was even tentatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.240
offered the role for a short amount of time.&nbsp;
Unfortunately for Brolin, Roger Moore decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.840
to come back to the franchise after all, killing&nbsp;
any dreams of an American Bond. Maybe one day.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.040
Having opened in 1935, Pinewood Studios has&nbsp;
been with Bond since the very beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.880
But during their 60 years of filming there,&nbsp;
the franchise's cast and crew have endured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.000
their fair share of disasters. In 1984, the&nbsp;
stage on which most of the Bond films were shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.080
tragically burned to the ground. It took a&nbsp;
full year to rebuild, and it was eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.200
renamed as the "Albert R. Broccoli 007&nbsp;
Stage," in honor of the legendary producer.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.520
Unfortunately, the 007 Stage burned down again&nbsp;
in 2006. At the time, the 007 Stage was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.280
being used for a Bond movie, and the blaze&nbsp;
ended up destroying the fake Venice used in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:09.680
Casino Royale. Luckily, the filmmakers had just&nbsp;
finished shooting, no one was hurt, and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.640
weren't any dangerous pyrotechnics or flammable&nbsp;
objects around to make things worse. Even so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.560
it took around 90 minutes to get the fire under&nbsp;
control. By 2007, the 007 Stage was open again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.000
newly enlarged, and now it's one of&nbsp;
the biggest sound stages in Europe.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.000
James Bond has always pulled out the&nbsp;
stops when it comes to theme songs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.600
Among the legendary artists who have written&nbsp;
for the franchise are Shirley Bassey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.880
Paul McCartney, Madonna, Gladys Knight,&nbsp;
Carly Simon, Tom Jones, and… Ace of Base?

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.600
Yes, indeed, the 90s Swedish pop group Ace of Base&nbsp;
was actually hired to write a Bond theme — for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.720
Pierce Brosnan's Bond debut, GoldenEye. Sadly,&nbsp;
the head honchos at Arista Records, the label&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.160
that represented the band, thought GoldenEye was&nbsp;
going to be a flop, and they didn't want their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.720
primo band associated with failure. So Arista&nbsp;
pulled Ace of Base from the project, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.520
James Bond folks went with Tina Turner instead.&nbsp;
However, the Swedish band did rename their track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.840
"The Juvenile," and if you're interested, you&nbsp;
can still hear the 007 song that could've been.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.840
A number of other famous artists have written Bond&nbsp;
themes over the years, too, only to fall at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.480
final hurdle. Blondie were locked-in to pen the&nbsp;
title track to For Your Eyes Only for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.040
Johnny Cash wrote his own version of Thunderball,&nbsp;
and Pulp almost did the theme for Tomorrow Never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.000
Dies. Even Radiohead almost got in on the Bond&nbsp;
action, writing a title track for Spectre that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.800
sadly, was rejected by producers — because it&nbsp;
was, in their opinion, too dark for the movie.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.200
After Sean Connery left the franchise, the&nbsp;
producers were adamant that they should find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.240
the perfect replacement for him. But&nbsp;
nobody could have guessed it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.360
George Lazenby , the Australian car mechanic who&nbsp;
fell into male modeling. According to Lazenby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.440
he had never acted in his life, but when he got&nbsp;
the info about auditions during a threesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.040
the man decided he would work as hard&nbsp;
as he could to win the producers over.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.840
Over the next four months, they made&nbsp;
him jump through a number of hoops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.920
including a screen test with a wrestler&nbsp;
who Lazenby accidentally knocked out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.880
Then, in a genuinely shocking move, the producers&nbsp;
sent him a sex worker to make sure he wasn't gay.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.840
"This never happened to the other fella."

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.320
Given that he allegedly ended up sleeping&nbsp;
with almost every girl on set, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.080
it seems like Lazenby passed that&nbsp;
particularly disturbing test.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.640
In fact, Lazenby actually did well with his&nbsp;
first Bond film, On Her Majesty's Secret Service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.440
and was even offered a $1 million contract to&nbsp;
star in six more movies… but his agent at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.440
time told him to turn it down. According&nbsp;
to his agent, the Bond age was over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.240
and people were more into gritty films, like&nbsp;
Easy Rider. So Lazenby left Bond behind — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.320
was eternally blackballed for being a nobody&nbsp;
that turned down the biggest franchise around.

00:06:33.828 --> 00:06:37.040
Roger Moore was a man of many&nbsp;
talents. Not only did he play 007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.040
but he has also written about the iconic&nbsp;
spy, too. In his book, Bond on Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.360
Moore gives ample insight into the world of Ian&nbsp;
Fleming's character. And while Moore provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.200
all sorts of interesting behind-the-scenes&nbsp;
details, he drops one informational nugget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.120
that's particularly surprising. Though&nbsp;
the franchise has flourished for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.760
Moore says that, during his time playing Bond,&nbsp;
he got by largely without the help of a script.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.000
Back in Moore's day, before shooting began,&nbsp;
the basic plot was laid out and the action set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
pieces were in place — but all that&nbsp;
snappy dialogue was constantly changing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.080
from the first read to the final shooting dates.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.720
Often, while on set with director Lewis Gilbert,&nbsp;
Moore would have his lines memorized and ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.960
to go — when Gilbert would suddenly decide they&nbsp;
could do better, and basically flush the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.920
down the toilet. And while Moore admits that this&nbsp;
was probably frustrating for the screenwriters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.720
he also suggests that the paychecks&nbsp;
were big enough to make up for it.

00:07:26.225 --> 00:07:29.600
Timothy Dalton was one of the top&nbsp;
choices to replace Sean Connery in 1969,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.560
but Dalton himself felt he was&nbsp;
too young for the role. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.560
the producers didn't forget his name, and by the&nbsp;
time Roger Moore decided to leave the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:40.160
they had swung back around to Dalton.&nbsp;
Since it'd been almost 20 years since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.760
the first request, Dalton finally felt&nbsp;
he was old enough to take on the role.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.920
The Welsh actor played a much less comedic&nbsp;
version of 007 in The Living Daylights and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.080
Licence to Kill, and he was all set to do a&nbsp;
third — but a legal dispute put the film on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.560
hold and released Dalton from his contractual&nbsp;
obligations. When the studio was ready to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.720
up the franchise again, Dalton had decided&nbsp;
he only wanted to return one last time — but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.160
after such a long wait between movies,&nbsp;
the producers wanted a Bond who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:07.600
stick around for a while. So Dalton&nbsp;
passed the torch to the next fella.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.240
While Pierce Brosnan first donned the James Bond&nbsp;
suit for GoldenEye, he was actually meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.880
show up a little bit earlier. The Irish actor was&nbsp;
first offered the role for The Living Daylights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.040
but he couldn't get out of his TV contract for&nbsp;
Remington Steele. Fortunately, the producers came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.200
back around to Brosnan when he was conflict-free.&nbsp;
But while he brought a new energy to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:30.000
franchise, Brosnan never felt happy with his&nbsp;
performance as Bond. As the man himself put it:

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.840
"I felt I was caught in a time warp between&nbsp;
Roger [Moore] and Sean [Connery]. [...] The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:37.520
violence was never real, the brute&nbsp;
force of the man was never palpable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.160
It was quite tame, and the characterization didn't&nbsp;
have a follow-through of reality, it was surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.520
But then that might have had to do with my&nbsp;
own insecurities in playing him as well."

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.000
As the latest actor to play James Bond,&nbsp;
Daniel Craig has plenty of mixed feelings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.000
about the iconic role. He takes the films&nbsp;
seriously and loves the work, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.520
but also once called the role "a drag"&nbsp;
and has occasionally insisted he wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.960
nothing less than to never play the&nbsp;
role again. More recently, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.440
he has walked back those comments, calling his&nbsp;
gig as the super spy the best job in the world.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.840
So what's up the mixed emotions? Well,&nbsp;
maybe Craig feels so conflicted because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.440
Bond is his complete opposite.&nbsp;
As the actor once told TimeOut:

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.480
"I was aware of what was needed to&nbsp;
be Bond, but it still goes against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.240
everything I believe in. You've met me&nbsp;
a couple of times, I'm not very cool."

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:24.320
But Bond has to be cool, he goes on to&nbsp;
say, and that often feels awkward for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:27.920
Though you'd never guess that Daniel Craig&nbsp;
thinks he's kind of a dork, it's easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.400
understand the pressure of playing the smoothest&nbsp;
character in film history. Still, if the popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.800
reception to his Bond is anything to go by, Craig&nbsp;
really doesn't have anything to worry about.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.720
[https://youtu.be/xsNIGJdxws8?t=114 1:54&nbsp;
to 1:58 - The name's Bond. James Bond."]

